THAILAND
Economically active population
The economically active population, or the Lao PDR's national labour force,
is a pivotal measure of a country's economic status and potential for growth.
Measuring the size and structure of a country's labour force can reflect the
degree to which human resources are engaged by the national economy and
can point to areas of inequality, skills or education mismatch with labour market
demands, and potential growth.
The economically active population figures presented here go beyond reflecting
formal wage-based employment to include foITTIS of self-employment or "own-account
employment" and work on family farms or enterprises which contributes to the country's
economy but does not involve the exchange of formal wages. It should be noted that
domestic work where household duties are performed without pay contributes significantly
to the broader functioning of society and the economy but is not included in the measure
of the economically active population. Meanwhile, in the Lao POR, a huge amount of
economic activity is performed on family farms or for small family businesses, thus
own-account employment is especially common and is included in the economically active
population.
The economically active population is defined as the population 10 years of age and
older which has engaged in daily work regularly at some point during the 12 months
prior to the census. Whereas economic activity is typically measured among populations
15 years of age and older, economic activity in the Lao PDR is still heavily tied to the
agricultural sector in which children 10 years and older often work. In 2005, 5% of the
Lao national labour force consisted of working children and 18% of children between 10
and 14 years of age were engaged in formal employment instead of attending school regularly
(Messerli et al., 2008). As in the 2005 Population and Housing Census, for every person 10 years
